{"success": true, "message": "Test details sent successfully", "data": {"test": {"_id": "69de21f17e39d99b57bc5b0c", "analysisVideoCdn": "yt", "exam": ["605dfcb2f226ac25a8bd5fef"], "ctype": null, "position": 0, "order": 12, "enableCalculator": false, "isContinuousQuestion": false, "reattemptAvailable": false, "singleSectionMultipleSubjects": false, "timeRestricted": false, "title": "Dual Nature of Matter - Test 3", "description": "Previous Year Questions as Chapter Test", "syllabus": "This test contains questions from all topics of this chapter included in the JEE Main syllabus. It includes a mix of questions from PYQs of JEE Main 2019 - 2025.", "totalTime": 50, "totalQuestions": 25, "totalMarks": 100, "bonusQuestions": null, "bonusMarks": null, "startDate": "2026-04-15T03:30:00.000Z", "endDate": "2027-04-30T00:00:00.000Z", "type": "free", "class": "605df635a37b7025c438d685", "instruction": "608289ffb160b022e9442c83", "subjects": [{"subjectId": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "totalMarks": 100, "bonusMarks": 0, "totalQuestions": 25, "bonusQuestions": 0, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd8ca"}], "sections": [{"sectionId": {"_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd8c7", "title": "Physics Single Correct", "sectionType": "normal", "subject": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "questions": [{"_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd847", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b475f"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4715"], "question": {"text": "A nucleus <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mo>,</mo></math> with a finite de-broglie wavelength <math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#955;</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></math> undergoes spontaneous fission into two nuclei <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math> and <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math> of equal mass. <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math> flies in the same direction as that of <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mo>,</mo></math> while <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math> flies in the opposite direction with a velocity equal to half of that of <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mo>.</mo></math> The de-Broglie wavelengths <math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#955;</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></mrow></msub><mi>&#160;</mi></math> and <math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#955;</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></mrow></msub></math> of B and C are respectively:"}, "options": [{"id": "8d1fce", "text": "<math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">λ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">λ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd848"}, {"id": "7e4c0e", "text": "<math><mfrac><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">λ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">λ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mrow></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd849"}, {"id": "b11bd8", "text": "<math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">λ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">λ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mrow></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd84a"}, {"id": "119762", "text": "<math><mn>2</mn><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">λ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">λ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mrow></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd84b"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd84c", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60d2d1d1185d7a32fa320586"], "concepts": ["6117b64d478ae845c43c6843"], "question": {"text": "In photoelectric effect an em-wave is incident on a metal surface and electrons are ejected from the surface. If the work function of the metal is 2.14 eV and stopping potential is 2 V , what is the wavelength of the em-wave ?\n(Given hc $=1242 \\mathrm{eVnm}$ where h is the Planck's constant and c is the speed of light in vaccum.)"}, "options": [{"id": "dbac7b", "text": "300 nm", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd84d"}, {"id": "451130", "text": "400 nm", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd84e"}, {"id": "f6cbc3", "text": "600 nm", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd84f"}, {"id": "a6b1a2", "text": "200 nm", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd850"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd851", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60d2d1d1185d7a32fa320586"], "concepts": ["60d2d1d0185d7a32fa320536"], "question": {"text": "<p>The threshold wavelength for photoelectric emission from a material is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5500</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mo>&#8728;</mo></mover></math>. Photoelectrons will be emitted, when this material is illuminated with monochromatic radiation from a</p><p>A.&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>75</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W</mi></math> infra-red lamp</p><p>B.&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W</mi></math> infra-red lamp</p><p>C.&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>75</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W</mi></math> ultra-violet lamp</p><p>D.&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W</mi></math> ultra-violet lamp</p><p>Choose the correct answer from the options given below :</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "e6313e", "text": "B and C only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd852"}, {"id": "fb09fa", "text": "A and D only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd853"}, {"id": "09b4e7", "text": "C only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd854"}, {"id": "ad39a8", "text": "C and D only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd855"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd856", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b475f"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4715"], "question": {"text": "What should be the order of arrangement of de-Broglie wavelength of electron <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></msub></mfenced></math>, an&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#945;</mi></math>-particle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#945;</mi></msub></mfenced></math> and proton <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></msub></mfenced></math> given that all have the same kinetic energy ?"}, "options": [{"id": "d44bde", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>&#945;</mi></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd857"}, {"id": "4d5f8a", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></msub><mo>&#60;</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></msub><mo>&#60;</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#945;</mi></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd858"}, {"id": "30498e", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#945;</mi></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd859"}, {"id": "543b75", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#945;</mi></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd85a"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd85b", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60d2d1d1185d7a32fa320586"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4706"], "question": {"text": "<p>For the photoelectric effect, the maximum kinetic energy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mfenced></math> of the photoelectrons is plotted against the frequency <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>)</mo></math> of the incident photons as shown in figure. The slope of the graph gives</p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2Fed1907e6-cf04-40e1-86a4-24ac978e0eec.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 250px; height: 181px;\"/>"}, "options": [{"id": "3df94b", "text": "Ratio of Planck’s constant to electric charge", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd85c"}, {"id": "484068", "text": "Work function of the metal", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd85d"}, {"id": "48244c", "text": "Charge of electron", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd85e"}, {"id": "121e17", "text": "Planck’s constant", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd85f"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd860", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b475f"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4715"], "question": {"text": "The de Broglie wavelengths for an electron and a photon are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></math> respectively. For the same kinetic energy of electron and photon, which of the following presents the correct relation between the de Broglie wavelengths of two?"}, "options": [{"id": "226551", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>&#8733;</mo><msubsup><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd861"}, {"id": "99aa25", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></msub><mo>&#8733;</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd862"}, {"id": "d58462", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>&#8733;</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd863"}, {"id": "d35b7c", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>&#8733;</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mfrac></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd864"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd865", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60d2d1d1185d7a32fa320586"], "concepts": ["6117b5e3478ae845c43c5df8"], "question": {"text": "In a photoelectric experiment, increasing the intensity of incident light:"}, "options": [{"id": "a489a2", "text": "increases the number of photons incident and also increases the K.E. of the ejected electrons.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd866"}, {"id": "aae9ba", "text": "increases the frequency of photons incident and increases the K.E. of the ejected electrons.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd867"}, {"id": "f8495b", "text": "increases the number of photons incident and the K.E. of the ejected electrons remains unchanged.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd868"}, {"id": "cfb488", "text": "increases the frequency of photons incident and the K.E. of the ejected electrons remains unchanged.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd869"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd86a", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b475f"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4715"], "question": {"text": "When photon of energy <math><mn>4.0</mn><mi></mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math> strikes the surface of a metal <math><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo></math> the ejected photoelectrons have maximum kinetic energy <math><msub><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math> and de-Broglie wavelength <math><msub><mrow><mi>&#955;</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>.</mo></math> The maximum kinetic energy of photoelectrons liberated from another metal <math><mi>B</mi></math> by photon of energy <math><mn>4.50</mn><mi></mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math> is <math><msub><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>-</mo><mn>1.5</mn></mrow></mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mo>.</mo></math> If the de-Broglie wavelength of these photoelectrons <math><msub><mrow><mi>&#955;</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mrow><mi>&#955;</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></math> then the work function of metal <math><mi>B</mi></math> is:"}, "options": [{"id": "1371d2", "text": "<math><mn>4</mn><mi></mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd86b"}, {"id": "4c3c68", "text": "<math><mn>2</mn><mi></mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd86c"}, {"id": "496c52", "text": "<math><mn>1.5</mn><mi></mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd86d"}, {"id": "217d1b", "text": "<math><mn>3</mn><mi></mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd86e"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd86f", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60d2d1d1185d7a32fa320586"], "concepts": ["60e2fb1e66f9ce3f243b4b10"], "question": {"text": "A parallel beam of light of wavelength <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>900</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>nm</mi></math> and intensity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>100</mn><msup><mi>Wm</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math> is incident on a surface perpendicular to the beam. The&nbsp;number of photons crossing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></math> area perpendicular to the beam in one second is"}, "options": [{"id": "ba9fe6", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>16</mn></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd870"}, {"id": "6bed45", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>16</mn></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd871"}, {"id": "7b3864", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>17</mn></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd872"}, {"id": "670670", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>20</mn></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd873"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd874", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b475f"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4715"], "question": {"text": "If the deBroglie wavelength of an electron is equal to $10^{-}$ times the wavelength of a photon of frequency $6 \\times 10^{14}$ $\\mathrm{Hz}$, then the speed of electron is equal to :\n\n(Speed of light $\\left.=3 \\times 10^{8} \\mathrm{~m} / \\mathrm{s}\\right)$\n\nPlanck's constant $=6.63 \\times 10^{-34} \\mathrm{~J} . \\mathrm{s}$\n\nMass of electron $\\left.=9.1 \\times 10^{-31} \\mathrm{~kg}\\right)$"}, "options": [{"id": "fb9233", "text": "$1.1 \\times 10^{6} \\mathrm{~m} / \\mathrm{s}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd875"}, {"id": "e474e0", "text": "$1.7 \\times 10^{6} \\mathrm{~m} / \\mathrm{s}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd876"}, {"id": "5b6c73", "text": "$1.8 \\times 10^{6} \\mathrm{~m} / \\mathrm{s}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd877"}, {"id": "c37d90", "text": "$1.45 \\times 10^{6} \\mathrm{~m} / \\mathrm{s}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd878"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd879", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60d2d1d1185d7a32fa320586"], "concepts": ["622dc7dbe656470a5fa29d22"], "question": {"text": "Average force exerted on a non-reflecting surface at normal incidence is $2.4 \\times 10^{-4} \\mathrm{~N}$. If $360 \\mathrm{~W} / \\mathrm{cm}^2$ is the light energy flux during span of 1 hour 30 minutes, Then the area of the surface is:"}, "options": [{"id": "c9624f", "text": "$0.2 \\mathrm{~m}^2$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd87a"}, {"id": "7698b3", "text": "$20 \\mathrm{~m}^2$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd87b"}, {"id": "2f638f", "text": "$0.1 \\mathrm{~m}^2$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd87c"}, {"id": "af2307", "text": "$0.02 \\mathrm{~m}^2$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd87d"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd87e", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b475f"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4715"], "question": {"text": "<p>A proton and an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#945;</mi></math>-particle are accelerated from rest by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math> potentials, respectively. The ratio of their de-Broglie wavelength is :</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "e97eca", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>:</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>1</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd87f"}, {"id": "380842", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>:</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>1</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd880"}, {"id": "11965d", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>:</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>1</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd881"}, {"id": "4a6cc9", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>16</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>:</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>1</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd882"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd883", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60d2d1d1185d7a32fa320586"], "concepts": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52b7"], "question": {"text": "<p>Given below are two statements:</p><p><strong>Statement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mo>:</mo></math></strong> Two photons having equal linear momenta have equal wavelengths.</p><p><strong>Statement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>II</mi><mo>:</mo></math></strong> If the wavelength of the photon is decreased, then the momentum and energy of a photon will also decrease.</p><p>In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "5cba5c", "text": "Both Statement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math> and Statement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>II</mi></math> are false", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd884"}, {"id": "18d5c7", "text": "Both Statement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math> and Statement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>II</mi></math> are true", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd885"}, {"id": "65dc35", "text": "Statement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math> is true but Statement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>II</mi></math> is false", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd886"}, {"id": "c4e8ef", "text": "Statement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math> is false but Statement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>II</mi></math> is true", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd887"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd888", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b475f"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4715"], "question": {"text": "Particle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mtext>m</mtext><mtext>A</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mtext>m</mtext><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math> moving along the <math><mtext>x</mtext></math> -axis with velocity <math><mrow><msub><mtext>v</mtext><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> collides elastically with another particle <math><mtext>B</mtext></math> at rest having mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mtext>m</mtext><mtext>B</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mtext>m</mtext><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math>. If both the particles move along the <math><mtext>x</mtext></math> -axis after the collision, the change <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8710;</mo><mi>&#955;</mi></math> in the wavelength of the particle <math><mtext>A</mtext></math>, in terms of its de-Broglie wavelength <math><mrow><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></mrow></math> before the collision is:"}, "options": [{"id": "07a31c", "text": "<math><mrow><mi>&#8710;</mi><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd889"}, {"id": "39b9a3", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#8710;</mi><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>5</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd88a"}, {"id": "6d13cd", "text": "<math><mrow><mi>&#8710;</mi><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd88b"}, {"id": "2ddca8", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#8710;</mi><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd88c"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd88d", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60d2d1d1185d7a32fa320586"], "concepts": ["60e2fb5666f9ce3f243b4f02"], "question": {"text": "Light is incident normally on a completely absorbing surface with an energy flux of <math><mi>&#160;</mi><mn>25</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math> . If the surface has an area of <math><mi>&#160;</mi><mn>25</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math> , the momentum transferred to the surface in <math> <mrow> <mtext>40</mtext><mtext>&#8201;</mtext><mtext>min</mtext></mrow> </math> time duration will be:"}, "options": [{"id": "f7ef52", "text": "<math><mn>6.3</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd88e"}, {"id": "a47703", "text": "<math><mn>5.0</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd88f"}, {"id": "c9e63e", "text": "<math><mn>3.5</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd890"}, {"id": "f024a7", "text": "<math><mn>1.4</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd891"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd892", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b475f"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4715"], "question": {"text": "An electron in the ground state of the hydrogen atom has the orbital radius of $5.3 \\times 10^{-11} \\mathrm{~m}$ while that for the electron in third excited state is $8.48 \\times 10^{-10} \\mathrm{~m}$. The ratio of the de Broglie wavelengths of electron in the excited state to that in the ground state is"}, "options": [{"id": "706cc5", "text": "3", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd893"}, {"id": "239980", "text": "16", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd894"}, {"id": "e064b9", "text": "9", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd895"}, {"id": "9e92e5", "text": "4", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd896"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd897", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60d2d1d1185d7a32fa320586"], "concepts": ["6117b64d478ae845c43c6843"], "question": {"text": "In an experiment with photoelectric effect, the stopping potential,"}, "options": [{"id": "e01b21", "text": "increases with increase in the intensity of the incident light", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd898"}, {"id": "5373c1", "text": "decreases with increase in the intensity of the incident light", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd899"}, {"id": "0111bd", "text": "increases with increase in the wavelength of the incident light", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd89a"}, {"id": "329574", "text": "is $\\left(\\frac{1}{\\mathrm{e}}\\right)$ times the maximum kinetic energy of the emitted photoelectrons", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd89b"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd89c", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b475f"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4715"], "question": {"text": "The ratio of wavelengths of proton and deuteron accelerated by potential <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></math>&nbsp;is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>:</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math>. Then, the ratio of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></math>&nbsp;to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></math>&nbsp;will be"}, "options": [{"id": "1606e9", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>1</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd89d"}, {"id": "06a669", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>:</mo><mn>1</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd89e"}, {"id": "00e1c1", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>1</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd89f"}, {"id": "def3d7", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>1</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd8a0"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd8a1", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60d2d1d1185d7a32fa320586"], "concepts": ["60d2d1d0185d7a32fa320536"], "question": {"text": "<p>Given below are two statements :</p><p><strong>Statement I :</strong> Stopping potential in photoelectric effect does not depend on the power of the light source.</p><p><strong>Statement II :</strong> For a given metal, the maximum kinetic energy of the photoelectron depends on the wavelength of the incident light.</p><p>In the light of above statements, choose the most&nbsp;appropriate answer from the options given below.</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "0397b6", "text": "Statement I is incorrect but statement II is correct", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd8a2"}, {"id": "78f357", "text": "Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd8a3"}, {"id": "4eb1c3", "text": "Statement I is correct but statement II is incorrect", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd8a4"}, {"id": "e4d3ec", "text": "Both statement I and statement II are correct", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd8a5"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f02c677dc36a88fd8a6", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b475f"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4715"], "question": {"text": "An electron (of mass <math><mi>m</mi></math> ) and a photon have the same energy <math><mi>E</mi></math> in the range of a few <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>. The ratio of the de-Broglie wavelength associated with the electron and the wavelength of the photon is ( <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mo>=</mo></math> speed of light in vacuum)"}, "options": [{"id": "ff2fec", "text": 